, including a 51-45 McGuire lead and a 46-45 lead from a Democratic-leaning pollster", "Expert ratings split between Likely R and Lean R", "Perriello's fundraising edge and Democratic enthusiasm signals in Virginia"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican edge: an R+6 district, an incumbent, and a rating consensus around Likely R, while the polling average sits near a five-point McGuire lead. The tightening trend and Perriello's cash advantage justify a modest shift toward the Democrat, but not enough to escape Lean R territory.", "watch": "Any public poll showing Perriello tied or ahead, or a strong Democratic statewide environment in Virginia, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent John McGuire (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican John McGuire defends a central Virginia district that leans Republican by six points, against Democrat Tom Perriello, a former congressman making a comeback bid. Most professional raters place the race between Likely R and Lean R, and prediction markets give McGuire roughly a three-in-four chance. Perriello has out-raised the incumbent and holds a large cash advantage, and Democratic surrogates have campaigned in the district.\n\nRecent polling shows the race tightening: a Washington Post/SSPG survey put McGuire up 51-45, while a Democratic-leaning pollster found a narrower 46-45 lead, with a hypothetical matchup showing Perriello slightly ahead. The polling average still favors McGuire by about five points, consistent with the district's fundamentals. The race is competitive but tilts Republican heading into Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1914, "margin": -5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2771, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2238, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2476, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2003, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1957, "margin": -